content:
Good, the test file exists. Now I have a comprehensive understanding. Let me write up my review.

Summary of findings:

**Critical: CSS Specificity Bug — Boundary Overflow Suppression Is Ineffective**

The boundary overflow suppression rules:
```css
.history-detail .history-step-header + .conv-record::before { top: 0; }
.history-detail .conv-record:has(+ .history-step-header)::before { bottom: 0; }
.history-detail > .conv-record:first-child::before { top: 0; }
.history-detail > .conv-record:last-child::before { bottom: 0; }
```

have lower specificity (0,3,1) than the base `::before` rule:
```css
.history-detail .conv-record[class*="step-type-"]:not(.step-status-row):not(.group-status-marker)::before {
  top: -7px;
  bottom: -7px;
  ...
}
```
which has specificity (0,5,1) due to the `[class*="step-type-"]` attribute selector and the two `:not()` pseudo-classes (each `:not(.class)` contributes the specificity of its class argument, (0,1,0)).

Since the base rule has higher specificity, its `top: -7px` and `bottom: -7px` always override the boundary rules' `top: 0` and `bottom: 0`. The lane background WILL overflow ±7px at step boundaries, which means the step-to-step boundary is visually altered — contradicting the task description's "step 与 step 之间的边界保持现状不变" requirement.

The fix: increase the specificity of the boundary suppression rules to at least match the base rule, by including the `[class*="step-type-"]` attribute selector and the `:not()` pseudo-classes.

Let me now format this as a proper review response.

